      "id": 1,
      "text": "Seward, Kansas lies within Seward County, which was established March 20, 1873 and named for William Seward, Lincoln’s Secretary of State.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seward_County%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The county seat of Seward County is Liberal, whose growth accelerated when the Chicago, Rock Island and Pacific Railroad rerouted through it after a bridge failure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seward_County%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Rock Island Depot and Hotel, built in 1910 in Liberal, is listed on the State Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kansashistory.gov/kansapedia/seward-county-kansas/15284",
